<section class="page-hero"><div class="ph-media"><img class="zoom" src="/assets/img/photos/tree-on-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/tree-on-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/tree-on-house.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="627" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="shell ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/">Services</a><span class="sep">›</span><span aria-current="page">Tree Strike &amp; Limb Damage Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Tree Damage Claim Appraisal in Tennessee</h1><p class="lede">Tennessee’s mature hardwoods are part of its character — until a storm drops one through the roof. Tree claims combine structural repair, crane work, debris removal and limited tree allowances, and each piece is a place where the estimate can come up short.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn outline-light" href="/get-a-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Common trees</dt><dd>Oak · poplar · pine</dd></div><div><dt>Big costs</dt><dd>Crane &amp; framing</dd></div><div><dt>Watch for</dt><dd>Tree allowances</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="shell layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why tree claims are tricky</h2>
<p>A tree on a house is several claims in one. There’s the cost to remove the tree from the structure — often with a crane on a tight lot. There’s the structural repair to rafters, trusses, walls and the roof deck. There’s interior damage from rain getting in before the roof was tarped. And there’s the separate, usually limited, allowance for removing the rest of the tree and other debris.</p>
<h2>Common shortfalls</h2>
<ul>
<li>Crane and rigging charges replaced with a generic “tree removal” line.</li>
<li>Framing repairs scoped as patches rather than engineered replacements.</li>
<li>Emergency tarping and board-up left out or underpaid.</li>
<li>Fence, deck, carport and outbuilding damage overlooked.</li>
<li>Debris removal allowances misapplied or not explained.</li>
<li>Interior water damage treated as unrelated to the tree strike.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Understanding tree allowances</h2>
<p>Many homeowner policies follow a common structure: debris removal for a tree that damages a covered structure is part of the loss, while removal of a fallen tree that hits nothing — or only blocks a driveway — is limited to a small per-tree amount with a cap per loss, or isn’t covered at all. Policy amounts vary, so check yours. Our <a href="/tennessee-claim-tools/tree-debris-allowance-calculator/">tree &amp; debris allowance calculator</a> walks through the common scenarios with illustrative numbers.</p>
<h2>Neighbors’ trees</h2>
<p>When a neighbor’s healthy tree falls on your house in a storm, your own policy usually responds. A neighbor’s insurer typically becomes involved only if the tree was obviously dead or diseased and the neighbor knew it. That’s a liability question, separate from valuing your damage.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is stump grinding covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually not. Policies typically pay to remove the tree debris, not to grind stumps or re-landscape.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays when my tree falls on my neighbor’s house?</summary><div class="a"><p>Generally the neighbor’s own homeowner policy covers their damage, unless the tree was visibly dead or hazardous and you were negligent.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need an engineer for framing damage?</summary><div class="a"><p>For significant structural damage, an engineer’s report helps establish the correct repair method and can be decisive in appraisal.</p></div></details></div>
